FILE: componentEngine.js
FEATURE: component-module
DATE: 2026-09-17
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'component-teardown'

REASON
NEW FILE: src/componentEngine.js

The template-instancing engine, shared by vUX-component.js and vUX-dataView.js.

It exists as its own file because sync() and DataView's reconcile() are the same
function. Shipping them twice would mean every future fix landing twice or, more
realistically, once. DataView's buildRow()/reconcile() were lifted here and
generalised from 'rows of a table' to 'any fragment', and DataView now delegates.

What it deliberately does not contain: observation, a scheduler, a virtual tree,
or an expression evaluator. Every binding value is a plain field name, optionally
negated with '!'. That constraint is the line between a binding layer and a
template language, and it is enforced here rather than left to documentation -
there is simply no code path that evaluates a string.

Design points worth recording:

  - matching() collects the root as well as its descendants. querySelectorAll
    alone misses the root, and a component whose only binding is on its outermost
    element is an ordinary thing to write.

  - Bindings are collected once per instance into a map, and a patch walks that
    map. A patch never re-queries the DOM.

  - Event handlers are bound through a wrapper that reads entry.data at call time
    rather than closing over it. A patch replaces entry.data wholesale, so a
    closure would hand the handler whatever the row held when it was first built.

  - An absent bound value removes the attribute rather than writing the string
    "undefined" into it, so a row without an avatar yields <img> with no src
    instead of a request for "undefined".

  - destroyInstance() detaches listeners explicitly even though removing the node
    would let the browser reclaim them, so that a consumer who keeps the node is
    not left with handlers firing against a destroyed instance.

  - spec.itemNoun exists solely so DataView keeps its original 'row object'
    wording in keying errors; the refactor changes no message a consumer may be
    matching on.


